3.1. System Overview
This chapter presents the architecture and design of the AR-Integrated Infrastructure Monitoring and Maintenance System (AR-IMMS), a platform that connects the physical layer of a Mini Data Center with a set of digital services for real-time monitoring, incident handling, and AR-assisted maintenance. The purpose of this section is to give a top-down view of the system before the individual components are examined in detail in the sections that follow.
At a high level, AR-IMMS is organized into five logical layers:
Physical layer – the Mini Data Center testbed, consisting of sites, rooms, racks, servers, and containerized workloads, each of which is instrumented with sensors/agents and, where applicable, a physical QR/ArUco marker for AR identification.
Data acquisition layer – lightweight Collector agents deployed on or near the physical servers that continuously read hardware and OS-level metrics (CPU, RAM, disk, network) and forward them as telemetry.
Backend layer – a NestJS application that receives telemetry, persists it, evaluates alert thresholds, manages the incident/ticket/maintenance workflow, enforces authentication and authorization, and exposes REST and WebSocket interfaces.
Data layer – a relational database that stores the infrastructure hierarchy, telemetry history, alerts, incidents, tickets, maintenance records, and audit logs.
Presentation layer – a Web Dashboard (Next.js) for administrators and operators, and a Mobile AR application (React Native) used by technicians in the field to identify equipment and view live telemetry overlaid on the physical device.
These layers communicate through well-defined interfaces: the Collector talks to the Backend over a telemetry channel, the Backend persists state in the database and publishes real-time updates through Socket.IO, and both the Web Dashboard and the Mobile AR client consume the Backend's REST API while subscribing to the same real-time channel for live updates. The overall goal of this architecture is to keep the Backend as the single source of truth, so that the digital twin shown on the Dashboard and the AR overlay shown to the technician are always consistent with the same underlying data.
The remaining sections of this chapter detail the overall system architecture (3.2), the responsibilities of each component (3.3), the communication mechanisms between them (3.4), the data flow through the system (3.5), the deployment topology (3.6), the security architecture (3.7), and the technology stack used to implement the system (3.8).
